{
  "summary": "Adding a spec review step between spec-writing and implementing is a strong idea. Currently, the pipeline reviews the plan (reviewing-plan) and the implementation (review), but specifications — which translate the high-level plan into concrete coding instructions — go unreviewed. This is a gap because specs can introduce errors, ambiguities, or omissions that the plan review couldn't catch (since specs didn't exist yet), and catching these before implementation is far cheaper than catching them in code review. The implementation would mirror the existing plan review pattern: a new 'reviewing-spec' stage between 'approved'→'spec-writing' and 'implementing', with a reviewer agent checking that the impl spec and test spec are complete, consistent with the plan, technically feasible, and don't have gaps in edge case coverage. Retry logic (similar to plan review's max 3 attempts) would send failed specs back to spec-writing with feedback. The main tradeoff is added pipeline latency, but since spec review would be quick (reviewing text, not code) and prevents costly implementation rework cycles, the net effect should be faster overall throughput."
}
